# Disable USB autosuspend on the hub carrying the keyboard/mouse receiver
# (Prolific 067b:2586, USB 2.0 Hub). 2026-08-01: found with autosuspend_delay_ms=0
# (suspends almost immediately on any pause in traffic) — this caused real
# resume-from-suspend latency on the mouse (Hoksi/DURGOD Taurus K320,
# connected downstream via port 1-13.4): smooth during fast continuous
# movement, but a perceptible stutter during slow/precise movement, since
# the natural pauses between micro-movements were enough to let the hub
# suspend, and each resume added a hitch before input reached the host.
#
# Also enables USB remote-wakeup on the hub itself. 2026-08-24: found that
# system suspend was not waking on keyboard/mouse input at all — the
# keyboard (1-13.4) and mouse dongle (1-13.1) both already had
# power/wakeup=enabled (kernel default for HID-class devices), but their
# parent hub (1-13, this device) had power/wakeup=disabled. Linux's USB
# remote-wakeup signal has to propagate up through every ancestor hub to
# reach the xHCI controller (already an enabled ACPI wake source, see
# 99-bluetooth-wakeup.rules) — a hub with wakeup disabled is a dead end
# for any wakeup event from its children, no matter how the children
# themselves are configured. Confirmed via journal: the box only ever
# woke from suspend via the physical power button, never via input.
#
# 2026-08-25 correction: enabling wakeup on the hub fixed real input but also
# opened the path for the mouse receiver's own chatter to wake the system.
# Confirmed via /sys/kernel/debug/wakeup_sources: after the 08-24 fix, the
# box self-woke from suspend after ~8-9 seconds four times overnight, each
# restarting powerdevil's 2h idle timer (looked like "unresponsive, needed a
# button press" — it wasn't failing to wake, it was waking itself constantly).
# hidpp_battery_0 (the Unifying receiver's HID++ battery/status channel,
# 046d:c547) had accumulated 203 wakeup events vs. 1 clean event each for the
# keyboard and the mouse's real HID interface — the receiver's own periodic
# telemetry, not user input, was the actual wake source. The keyboard
# (2f68:0082, wired, no telemetry channel) shows no equivalent chatter.
# Disabling wakeup on just the receiver (leaving the hub and keyboard
# enabled) trades away mouse-click wake for a suspend that reliably stays
# asleep and still wakes on keyboard input.
#
# 2026-08-28 REVERTED: this hub is a KVM switch (confirmed with Alex), not a
# plain peripheral hub -- the "keyboard"/"mouse" behind it are the switch's
# own USB presentation, not direct devices. With hub wakeup=enabled, Alex
# reported the screen going black on essentially every touch of the
# keyboard/mouse during normal (non-suspended) use -- strong circumstantial
# fit for the KVM switch reacting to wakeup/reset signaling by hiccupping
# its video pass-through too. Reverting hub wakeup to disabled trades away
# wake-from-suspend-via-keyboard (the point of the 08-24 fix) for a switch
# that doesn't glitch the display during active use, which is the only
# acceptable side. TEST==power/wakeup dropped since the target is now
# default/disabled and this device shouldn't be setting the attribute at
# all; the ATTR{power/control}="on" line (autosuspend fix, unrelated) stays.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="067b", ATTR{idProduct}=="2586", TEST=="power/control", ATTR{power/control}="on"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="046d", ATTR{idProduct}=="c547", TEST=="power/wakeup", ATTR{power/wakeup}="disabled"
